{"table":"nodes","ref":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/scalar.fixed_strip_error_budget","record":{"id":"scalar.fixed_strip_error_budget","kind":"claim","label":"Fixed-strip scalar accuracy with its cutoff dependence exposed","status":"unverified","source_id":"scalar-r2-26-source-consequences","locator":"consequences.tex:124–196; long reasoning record §7.1, ordinary quantitative deduction","statement":"For each fixed admissible minimizer gamma and cutoff 0<T<1, finite constants C_(gamma,T), N0_(gamma,T) give |S_(T,N)-P_star|<=(1-T)m(T)+C_(gamma,T)sqrt(T/N) for N>=N0, where m(T)=E Phi_xx(T,X_T). The adjacent sum retains the rounding coordinate. Monotone quadrature costs at most Delta and weighted Gronwall uses exp(K_T integral_0^T gamma)<=exp(K_T).","assumption_ids":["scalar.paper_minimizer"],"citation_ids":["scalar-r2-26-cite-euler-normalization","scalar-r2-26-cite-correlation-vector-control","scalar-r2-26-cite-terminal-loss","support_regularity_source"],"evidence_ids":["ordinary_reconstruction_bounds_20261024","ordinary_deductions_review_20261026_r9"],"boundary":"Ordinary deduction from fixed-strip spatial and temporal bounds, maximal coupled Euler estimates, Bessel control and the terminal tail. Monotone gamma may have jumps; the proof uses its total variation, not its derivative. Constants need not remain bounded as T approaches one, and no effective numerical minimizer bounds or arbitrary prescribed diagonal rate are asserted. This rate is not a selected Lean conclusion."},"usage":{"record_ref":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/scalar.fixed_strip_error_budget","paper_focus":[],"paper_memberships":[{"stage":"long","subgraph_ref":"sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json/support/0.3.1/2f85413164f06365ea0d825befee447650da71d54080d5dd65e78a708891c560","roles":["mapped_claim"]}],"direct_passages":[{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:c4249feffa26e48c24c5690f73e0de4886e40504c8c5c0e6dcb22ec94ea334da/papers/long.md/sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json/passages/gaussian-error-budget","anchor":"gaussian-error-budget","mapping_ref":"sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids"}]},"aligned_passages":[{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:c4249feffa26e48c24c5690f73e0de4886e40504c8c5c0e6dcb22ec94ea334da/papers/long.md/sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json/passages/gaussian-error-budget","anchor":"gaussian-error-budget","mapping_ref":"sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids","text":"\n\n<a id=\"scalar-error-budget\"></a>\n\n### 7.1 Error estimates at a fixed cutoff\n\nFix the minimizer first and then $T<1$. The bounds below have constants\ndepending on that minimizer and strip; they give an $N^{-1/2}$ rate there,\nnot a rate uniform as $T\\uparrow1$. Those derivative bounds are finite\nbut have not been supplied as numerical inputs to an algorithm for choosing\na mesh. The calculation is an ordinary deduction from the source's strip\nestimates, beyond the qualitative selected formal statement. Write\n$S_{T,N}=\\sum_{j=0}^N A_jB_{j+1}$ and\n$f(t)=\\mathbb E a(t,X_t)$, so $0\\le f(t)\\le1$. On the fixed strip let\n$K_T=\\sup_{t\\le T,x}|a(t,x)|$ and choose a spatial Lipschitz constant\n$L_T$ for $a$. Define\n$$\n \\delta_N=\\max_{0\\le j\\le N}\\|Y_j-X_{t_j}\\|_2,\\qquad\n d_N=\\max_{1\\le j\\le N}\n \\left|\\mathbb E a(t_{j-1},Y_{j-1})^2-1\\right|.\n$$\nThese errors have a rate at each fixed strip, even if $\\gamma$ has jumps.\nPut $\\Gamma=\\gamma(T)$ and $V=\\gamma(T)-\\gamma(0)$. Bounded spatial\nderivatives and the PDE give temporal Lipschitz constants\n$$\nJ=\\tfrac12\\|u_{xx}\\|_\\infty+\\Gamma K_T,\\qquad\nH=\\tfrac12\\|a_{xx}\\|_\\infty+\\Gamma(K_T^2+L_T)\n$$\nfor $u$ and $a$, respectively, on this strip. No derivative of $\\gamma$\nis used. Brownian coupling gives\n$\\|X_s-X_{\\pi_N(s)}\\|_2\\le\\sqrt\\Delta+\\Gamma\\Delta$.\nSubtract the Euler and diffusion equations and split the drift difference\non $[t_j,t_{j+1}]$ as\n$$\n\\gamma(s)\\bigl(u(s,X_s)-u(t_j,Y_j)\\bigr)\n +\\bigl(\\gamma(s)-\\gamma(t_j)\\bigr)u(t_j,Y_j).\n$$\nPut $A_T=\\int_0^T\\gamma(s)\\,ds\\le1$ and\n$w_j=\\int_{t_j}^{t_{j+1}}\\gamma(s)\\,ds$. Taking a pathwise maximum\nover grid times, Minkowski and discrete Gronwall give, with\n$$\nR_N=A_TK_T\\sqrt\\Delta+\n [A_T(J+\\Gamma K_T)+V]\\Delta,\n$$\nthe bound\n$$\n\\delta_N\\le\\left\\|\\max_{j\\le N}|Y_j-X_{t_j}|\\right\\|_2\n\\le e^{K_TA_T}R_N\\le e^{K_T}R_N.\n$$\nThe recurrence weights the preceding maximal norm on interval $j$ by\n$K_Tw_j$; their sum is $K_TA_T$. The term $V\\Delta$ is the monotone\nquadrature bound for the coefficient. For the\nmartingale residual, Itô isometry and the same increment estimate give\n$$\ne_N\\le K_T\\delta_N+\n\\sqrt T\\{L_T\\delta_N+L_T\\sqrt\\Delta+(H+\\Gamma L_T)\\Delta\\}.\n$$\nThus $\\delta_N,e_N=O_{\\gamma,T}(\\sqrt\\Delta)$.\nThese are deductions from the compact-strip bounds and stochastic\nidentities, not quantitative clauses of the selected Lean statement.\n\nSince $a$ is bounded and Lipschitz,\n$d_N\\le2K_TL_T\\delta_N\\to0$. Whenever $d_N\\le1/2$,\n$$\n \\sqrt{2/3}\\le c_j\\le\\sqrt2,\\qquad\n |1/c_j-1|\\le d_N,\\qquad\n \\|\\alpha\\|_2\\le\\sqrt{T(1+d_N)}.\n$$\nThese estimates give an explicit sufficient condition for all the required\nnormalisers to be positive. They do not assume positivity for every\ncoarse mesh.\n\nThe first-moment identity in §6 makes $f=m$ nonincreasing, with\n$f(0)=1$. Its right Riemann sum therefore satisfies\n$$\n0\\le I(T)-\\Delta\\sum_{j=1}^N f(t_j)\n\\le\\Delta\\bigl(f(0)-f(T)\\bigr).\n$$\nRemoving the last term adds $\\Delta f(T)$, so\n$0\\le I(T)-\\Delta\\sum_{j=1}^{N-1}f(t_j)\\le\\Delta$.\nThe difference between the shifted interior sum and the integral is bounded by\n$$\n \\left|\\Delta\\sum_{j=1}^{N-1}\n       \\frac{\\mathbb E a(t_j,Y_j)}{c_j}-\\int_0^T f(t)\\,dt\\right|\n \\le TK_Td_N+TL_T\\delta_N+\\Delta.\n$$\nThe first two terms replace the normalisers and Euler positions; the\nlast is the preceding monotone quadrature estimate.\nCombining this with the vector and rounding-endpoint estimate gives\n$$\n \\begin{aligned}\n |S_{T,N}-P_*|\n \\le{}&(1-T)m(T)\n   +\\bigl(2+\\sqrt{T(1+d_N)}\\bigr)e_N\n   +\\sqrt{\\Delta(1+d_N)}\\\\\n &+TK_Td_N+TL_T\\delta_N+\\Delta .\n \\end{aligned}\n$$\nIn particular, for each fixed minimizer and $T<1$ there are finite\n$C_{\\gamma,T}$ and $N_0(\\gamma,T)$ such that\n$$\n|S_{T,N}-P_*|\\le(1-T)m(T)+C_{\\gamma,T}\\sqrt{T/N}\n\\qquad(N\\ge N_0(\\gamma,T)).\n$$\nThe constants are not uniform as $T\\uparrow1$. The\n[coefficient budget](#coefficient-budget) bounds $\\int\\gamma$ and\n$\\gamma$ on each fixed strip, but numerical bounds for all strip\nderivative constants and an effective mesh threshold are not supplied.\nThe scalar tail bound $(1-T)m(T)\\le1-T$ uses only monotonicity of\nthe first moment; Proposition 6.2 further makes it $o(1-T)$.\nAll terms after this scalar tail\ntend to zero at each fixed $T<1$. Given a scalar\ntolerance $\\zeta>0$, first choose $T$ with $1-T<\\zeta/2$, then choose\n$N_0(\\gamma,T,\\zeta)$ so that their sum is below $\\zeta/2$ for every\n$N\\ge N_0$. This is the required finite choice of a Gaussian spin.\nThe formula exposes the errors; it does not supply numerical values of\n$K_T,L_T,\\delta_N,e_N$ or an effective mesh threshold.\n[OpenAI (2026a), Lemma 2.1][fs-reg];\n[OpenAI (2026a), Proposition 7.2][fs-cons].\n\nA diagonal can be chosen using these thresholds. For prescribed\n$T_m\\uparrow1$, choose increasing $N_m$ large enough to make the\nmesh-dependent part of the fixed-$T_m$ bound less than $1/m$. An arbitrary increasing mesh schedule\ndoes not follow from the iterated limit alone. For example, the scalar\narray\n$$\n h(T,N)=\\min\\{1,[N(1-T)]^{-1}\\}\n$$\ntends to zero in $N$ at every fixed $T<1$, but\n$h(1-1/N,N)=1$ for every $N\\ge2$. This is an illustration of the\nquantifier issue, not an asserted error law for the SK construction.\n","span":{"start_byte":97892,"end_byte":103033,"start_line":2220,"end_line":2350},"paper_href":"/api/v1/papers/long?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20","reading_href":"/api/v1/papers/reading?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&paper=long&passage=gaussian-error-budget","query_href":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&paper=long&passage=gaussian-error-budget"}],"lenses":{"prerequisites":"/api/v1/graph?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&view=arguments&node=scalar.fixed_strip_error_budget&assumptions=true","consumers":"/api/v1/graph?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&view=arguments&node=scalar.fixed_strip_error_budget&direction=consumers&assumptions=true","direct_passages":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&node=scalar.fixed_strip_error_budget&relation=direct","prerequisite_uses":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&node=scalar.fixed_strip_error_budget&relation=prerequisites"},"relations":[{"family":"arguments","id":"field:c7fa5083c234cd3d7bc3a635c0b702ababc7392f4d9e67dd70d9fa1279fb5a54","from":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/scalar.fixed_strip_error_budget","to":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/scalar.paper_minimizer","kind":"assumption_ids","semantic_class":"explicit_assumption_reference","derived":true,"owner_ref":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/scalar.fixed_st